joined now by the minister for the  middle east and africa james cleverley  representing the government this morning  hello to you mr cleverley thank you for  joining us on the programme um what is  the plan to save the gas industry  well quasi curtin the  energy  minister the business minister has had a  series of meetings with uh the industry  to ensure  that the uh consumers in here in the uk  are protected and they're protected both  from  uh the increase potentially in gas  prices through the energy price cap that  we have in place but also to ensure that  we have continuity of supply and the  message that he's put out and he put out  a series of tweets that people can look  at if they wish to  is that because of the mix of where we  get our energy from we are in a better  place than many other countries that are  also feeding the pinch from this  increase in wholesale gas supply  and that we will absolutely do  everything we can to protect consumers  to make sure we have a vibrant  energy supply market  55 businesses i believe in the sector  according to this morning's uh front  page of the financial times there could  only be six to ten left by the end of  the year that according to the industry  this is pretty grim stuff  well as i say uh the the business  secretary has  already  had a series of meetings uh he will be  having others i am certain  and what we are doing is working to  ensure that we do two things and that is  to protect consumers from what has been  a a global increase in gas prices  but also to ensure the  security of energy supply and energy  provision  to those consumers and  we're in a pretty good place here in the  uk because of course we have our own  domestic gas supply  but we also receive gas from really very  reliable partners like norway for  example so we're in a better place than  many other countries around the world  will the government bear like small  suppliers who are in danger of going  bust  well as i say the the business secretary  has had and will continue to have  meetings with the sector  the priority is to make sure that we  protect provision for consumers and to  ensure that they aren't hit by  uh  unforeseeable or unpredictable a  significant increase in gas prices  and and those discussions will be  ongoing exactly how we do that will be  up for uh discussion of course but those  are our priorities so that's not on no  then is it i mean if if we see all these  businesses starting to potentially go  bust in order to try to protect people's  gas supplies the government's going to  have to step in and help them aren't  there  well as i say i'm not going to speculate  as to exactly how  we do this but i can assure you that the  conversations that the business  secretary has already had  have been very uh productive and we will  ensure we will ensure the protection of  consumers as i say from the price  inflation that we're seeing all over the  world but also we're very  very conscious of the importance of uh  protecting that energy supply as well  so again the article that i'm referring  to i'm sure you've seen it on the front  of the ft this morning talks about a  northern style  bad bank where um if there are lots of  people whose gas suppliers are going  under they all need to be put under one  umbrella protected by the state in order  to make sure that all of the other  businesses don't go under as well i'm  guessing that just gov good governance  means that you have to be looking at  something like that  well as i say we're we're considering  the options and i'm i'm not  uh i'm not gonna you know speculate as  to exactly how we addressed this but  we've moved quickly the business  secretaries say has already had a series  of meetings uh about this both last week  uh and over the weekend and we've got a  very clear idea of what our priorities  need to be in terms of protecting  consumers and protecting uh the security  of our energy supply so you're not  ruling it out then  okay you keep asking me and i keep  giving you the same answer we are  considering a range of options the  business sector has met with the uh um  the energy supply companies and our  priorities are to protect consumers and  also to protect the security of our  energy supply and we'll look we'll look  at a range of options no doubt but i'm  not going to rule anything in or commit  to anything here on  your show this morning i'm afraid okay  let me drive from a different angle i'm  sure my viewers very anxious this  morning about what's going to happen as  far as their gas is concerned if they or  energy bills are concerned when they  hear that of 55 companies only six out  of six to ten could be left standing by  the end of the year  how  potentially might the public be expected  to pay for that there is a an energy  price cap of 139 pounds for next month  um could that go higher  well the energy price cap is there not  to  uh completely prevents  energy prices ever going up but it's  about making sure that there are no  sudden unexpected unpredictable  big hikes in prices so the energy price  cap has always been designed  designed to move gently upwards but the  big thing is to prevent  as i say big unpredictable spikes in  consumer prices the other thing of  course that the government has put in  there's a whole series of  measures to protect the poorest families  uh in the country through things like um  [Music]  winter  winter fuel support  and also to make it easier for people to  switch providers to get the best deals  so as i say our priority remains to  protect consumers and also to protect  the security of our energy supply so  just clarify that 139 pound lift  cap for next month that is going to stay  at that until six months time when you  look at it again  yeah as i say it's about providing  predictability  for consumers and that the energy price  cap policy is in place it has protected  hundreds of thousands of consumers  already and will continue to do so okay  how worried are we about food supplies  um and the like and concerns about  carbon dioxide it's a little bit  complicated but just for the benefit of  our viewers this morning um we haven't  got enough carbon dioxide it could mean  that  animal husbandry is a challenge over the  next couple of weeks and also some  operations within the nhs and i found it  surprising that you need carbon dioxide  for some nhs hospital operations  apparently but but you do um  what is being put in place by the  government to make sure that that supply  is sorted out  well the  the the points that you raise uh once  again we find ourselves  in a situation that many countries in  the world are experiencing um the  the logistics industry which underpins a  lot of the movement of uh products like  co2 we we know the uh the situation  there and what we're looking to do is  twofold firstly is to make sure that in  the in the medium term and beyond that  the uk is increasingly self-sufficient  in terms of things like food production  but also in terms of uh people in the  logistics change that chain hgb drivers  that kind of stuff  and in the shorter term obviously we are  looking across government at what we  need to do to address what might be some  uh short-term shortages in these things  again these are things that we're  discussing we're discussing with the  industry we discuss them across  government  and we will ensure that we are able to  put food on the tables obviously that is  a real priority  and as i say we're looking across  government and how we address this okay  so alex sharma has been talking about it  as you say you've quoted him several  times during the course of the interview  he's pretty relaxed about things at the  moment prime minister on his way to new  york less relaxed saying that there will  be some supply shortages where are you  sitting on that scale  well we we already know that uh right  across the world we have seen uh for  example htv drivers um and it's not  unique to the uk a shortage of hgv  drivers as i say we are taking measures  to uh address that and you say about the  uh the prime minister uh traveling to  new york and a big part of the  uh the work he is going to do there is  actually to get the world focused on  some of the issues that we have to  address collectively to make sure that  we have things like food security in the  future and addressing uh climate change  is a really important part of that and  that's very much part of the  conversations that he'll be having over  in new york at the united nations  general assembly

